  "inputs_hash": "a3f165f0a7aaa59312d714594dd2a75ab894b26820bdf7668bd392caa8cd7c30",
  "output_hash": "7de5369c2d3014eb1940d26fa6d7b495a4cb5fbe69666e5838910fc0806b7438",
  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch02.s13",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch02.s13.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p01.01",
            "text": "How can a token embedding lookup be viewed as an ordinary neural-network operation, and why does that matter when turning token IDs into vectors?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p01.02",
            "text": "An embedding layer is mathematically the same as a neural network linear layer.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3049",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3051",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3198",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3199"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p01.03",
            "text": "To reproduce an embedding lookup with a linear layer, start from token IDs such as 2, 3, and 1, convert them into one-hot representations, and feed those vectors into a neural network.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3043",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3072",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3074",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3082",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3085",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3086",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3089",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3091",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3207",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3210",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3212"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p01.04",
            "text": "The network uses as many neurons as the embedding dimension, so in the example with five-dimensional embeddings it has five neurons.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3099",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3102",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3214",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3217"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p01.05",
            "text": "For this linear layer, the output is given by X multiplied by W transpose.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3102",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3104",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3144",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3149",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3219",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3221"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.s13.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p02.r01",
            "text": "This formulation also makes the output shape easy to interpret.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p02.r02",
            "text": "When there are three input tokens and five embedding dimensions, X multiplied by W transpose produces a matrix with three rows and five columns.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3152",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3154",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3158",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3168",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3170",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3172"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p02.r03",
            "text": "There are three rows because there are three inputs, and each row corresponds to the vector embedding for one particular input.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3158",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3160",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3165",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3172",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3175",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3177"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p02.r04",
            "text": "There are five columns because each input is mapped to a vector embedding with five dimensions.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3165",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3168",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3170",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3214",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3217"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p02.r05",
            "text": "This linear-layer construction produces the same output as the embedding layer.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3183",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3185",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3187",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3190",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3221",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3224",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3243",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3247",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3501",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3505"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.s13.p03",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p03.01",
            "text": "Even so, the equivalent formulation is not the one normally used for embeddings.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p03.02",
            "text": "The embedding layer is preferred because it is much more computationally efficient than using a linear layer on one-hot inputs.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3249",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3250",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3291",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3295",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3508",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3511"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p03.03",
            "text": "A linear layer is less efficient here because one-hot encoding introduces many zeros, which leads to many unnecessary multiplications with zero and other unnecessary computations.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3250",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3255",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3260",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3262",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3264",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3266",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3268"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p03.04",
            "text": "Those unnecessary computations scale up when the vocabulary is very large, such as GPT-2's vocabulary size.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3268",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3270",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3273",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3275"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s13.p03.05",
            "text": "So although a linear layer can be used to create the embedding matrix, the embedding layer is the preferred implementation.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3286",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3289",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3291",
              "ghCSGRgVB_o@3295"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
